% The Café Paradox, direct reading.
%
% This encoding argues directly: Bob cooks the meals served in the café, the café
% is operated by his household, therefore his household cooks — no detour
% through who eats there.  Under this reading household_cooks is derived
% outright from who cooks what, the program is stratified, and the paradox
% surfaces in the DATA rather than in the rules: Bob eats in the café (it
% is his home, and his household cooks), violating the policy's conclusion
% that everyone who takes meals in the café does not have a household
% that cooks.  The rule itself is ok — the problem arises from the
% situation in which it is applied.
%
% The alternative, self-referential reading — where whether a household
% cooks depends on where its members eat — is in cafe_paradox.dl.

% ------------------------------------------------------------------- facts
member(alice, house_a).
member(alan,  house_a).
member(carol, house_b).
member(bob,   cafe_house).

cafe(cafe_house).            % the café is operated by (is) this household
cooks_at_home(alice).        % alice cooks house_a's meals at home
cooks_cafe_meals(bob).       % bob cooks the meals served in the café

% ------------------------------------------------------------------- rules
% A household cooks its own meals if a member cooks at home ...
household_cooks(H) :- member(P, H), cooks_at_home(P).
% ... or, directly: it operates the café and one of its own members cooks
% the café's meals.  (Bob's situation exactly.)
household_cooks(H) :- cafe(H), cooks_cafe_meals(P), member(P, H).

% Where people take their meals.  One relation, because for Bob "at home"
% and "in the café" are the same place — his home IS the café.
eats_at(P, H) :- member(P, H), household_cooks(H).              % at home
eats_at(P, C) :- cafe(C), member(P, H), not household_cooks(H). % free café meals

% The policy's conclusion as an integrity check: "everyone who takes meals in
% the café does not have a household that cooks."  Nonempty = paradox.
violation(P) :- eats_at(P, C), cafe(C), member(P, H), household_cooks(H).
